# Prerequisites
*.d

# Object files
*.o
*.ko
*.obj
*.elf

# Linker output
*.ilk
*.map
*.exp

# ...but runtime/ holds product data, not build output, and two of the
# patterns above reach into it. `*.map` matches extensions.map, which is the
# extension-to-language table (HLR-060) and must ship; `*.so` matches the
# grammars, which must not, because the build produces them from pinned
# upstream releases. The negation is only for the former.
#
# An ignore rule that swallows a data file is silent: the file works locally,
# is absent from the clone CI makes, and the failure names the missing file
# rather than the rule. Anything added under runtime/ is worth a
# `git check-ignore -v` before it is relied on.
!runtime/extensions.map

# Precompiled Headers
*.gch
*.pch

# Libraries
*.lib
*.a
*.la
*.lo

# Shared objects (inc. Windows DLLs)
*.dll
*.so
*.so.*
*.dylib

# Executables
*.exe
*.out
*.app
*.i*86
*.x86_64
*.hex

# Debug files
*.dSYM/
*.su
*.idb
*.pdb

# Kernel Module Compile Results
*.mod*
*.cmd
.tmp_versions/
modules.order
Module.symvers
Mkfile.old
dkms.conf

# debug information files
*.dwo

# Build output
/build/

# Python bytecode from the spec tooling
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Reports elc writes over its own tree. `elc -o report.md .` is the ordinary
# way to look at a change before committing it, and the run leaves its output
# and companions behind in the working directory.
#
# Anchored to the root so the patterns cannot reach a `report.md` someone
# adds under doc/ later — the silent-swallow failure the runtime/ note above
# describes.
/report.md
/report.dot
